    h1: "What Drawings Need an Engineer Stamp for Texas Commercial Work?",
    answer: "The direct answer: in Texas, the structural, mechanical/electrical/plumbing (MEP), and civil drawing sets for nearly all commercial, industrial, and multi-family projects must carry the stamp of a Texas-licensed professional engineer in responsible charge. Assembly, educational, and institutional occupancies trigger the requirement regardless of size. The main carve-outs are one- and two-family dwellings designed to prescriptive code provisions and certain agricultural buildings — but the moment the design leaves the prescriptive path, the stamp requirement returns. Which exact sheets need sealing varies with occupancy, building size, and the authority having jurisdiction's amendments.\n\nThe drawing sets break down by discipline. Structural sheets include foundation plans, floor and roof framing plans, lateral-force-resisting system details, and connection schedules — the drawings that show how the building stands up and resists wind and seismic loads. MEP sheets include mechanical plans with load calculations and equipment schedules, electrical plans with one-line diagrams, panel schedules, and lighting layouts, and plumbing plans with riser diagrams and isometrics. Civil site sheets cover grading, drainage, paving, and on-site utilities. Energy compliance documentation — typically COMcheck or equivalent IECC compliance forms — rounds out the permit set. Fire protection shop drawings are often deferred submittals, but the engineer of record coordinates them so the deferred package matches the sealed design.\n\nTexas adds its own triggers on top of the base requirement. Along the Gulf Coast, the Texas Department of Insurance windstorm program requires windstorm-resistant design and inspection for structures in the designated catastrophe area, with a windstorm certificate (WPI-8) issued before the building final — the engineer designs to the TDI wind speeds and documents the inspections during construction. In the Dallas and Austin corridors, expansive clay soils make the geotechnical report a plan-check focal point: the foundation drawings must reflect the report's recommendations, whether drilled piers, post-tensioned slabs, or over-excavation. In the Panhandle, high-wind detailing drives the structural sheets. Houston's lack of zoning doesn't change the stamp requirement — the building permit and plan review still demand sealed drawings — but it does compress the entitlement timeline.\n\nThe gray areas are where projects stall. As-built drawings cannot simply be stamped as design documents: a PE seal certifies engineering work the engineer controlled, so as-builts need field verification and an engineering review before any seal goes on them. Tenant improvements inside an existing building still require sealed structural and MEP sheets whenever framing, load paths, or mechanical, electrical, or plumbing systems change — cosmetic-only TI work is the narrow exception. Residential remodels generally don't need an engineer stamp unless the work alters the structural system beyond prescriptive limits. When in doubt, the building official's intake checklist for your specific jurisdiction is the final word on which sheets need a seal.",
