    h1: "What Drawings Need an Engineer Stamp for Utah Commercial Work?",
    answer: "The direct answer: in Utah, the structural, MEP, and civil drawing sets for commercial, industrial, and multi-family projects must carry the seal of a Utah-licensed professional engineer in responsible charge — and significant structures additionally require Utah's Professional Structural Engineer designation. One- and two-family dwellings within prescriptive code limits are the principal exception. Which sheets the building department expects sealed depends on occupancy, structure type, and the amendments of the city or county reviewing the project.\n\nThe drawing sets carry Utah's two-hazard signature. Structural sheets show foundation plans, floor and roof framing, the lateral-force-resisting system with seismic detailing, and connection schedules — with the seismic design category and site-specific ground motion values on the cover sheet, plus ground snow loads, flat-roof snow loads, and drift provisions the reviewer checks against the jurisdiction's adopted values. MEP sheets include mechanical plans with load calculations, electrical one-lines and panel schedules, and plumbing risers. Civil site sheets cover grading, drainage, utilities, and stormwater, with bench developments adding slope-stability scope. Energy compliance documentation under the adopted IECC edition completes the set.\n\nUtah's signature trigger is the Wasatch Fault. The fault's earthquake hazard places much of the urban corridor — Salt Lake City, West Valley City, Sandy, Murray, Ogden, Provo — in high seismic design categories, driving the lateral system selection and the detailing the category demands. The geotechnical investigation is essential: valley soils carry liquefaction risk the foundation design must address, and foothill bench sites add slope stability. Snow is the second trigger: the valley floor carries moderate ground snow loads while bench and mountain jurisdictions — Park City, the Cottonwood corridors — adopt values several times higher, with drift and sliding snow analysis the reviewer expects. The structural drawings must show both hazards resolved, because Utah designs for them simultaneously.\n\nThe gray areas follow the national pattern. As-built drawings need field verification and engineering review before any Utah seal — the seal certifies controlled engineering work, not drafting. Tenant improvements require sealed structural and MEP sheets when systems or load paths change; finish-only TI is the narrow exception. Single-family homes within prescriptive limits generally don't need a stamp, but custom homes on benches, with long spans, or on liquefiable soils routinely do. The intake checklist at your city or county building department settles which sheets need sealing for your specific site.",
    directAnswer: "In Utah, a Utah-licensed PE — Professional Structural Engineer for significant structures — must stamp the structural, MEP, and civil drawing sets for commercial work. The Wasatch Fault drives seismic design while jurisdiction-specific snow loads shape every mountain and bench project.",
    topic: "PE Stamping by State",
